\begin{thm}
Theorem 2.2. A subset of \(R^n\) is convex if and only if it contains all the convex combinations of its elements.
\end{thm}

\begin{thm}
Theorem 2.3.
For any \( S \subset \mathbb{R}^n \), \( \text{conv } S \) consists of all the convex combinations of the elements of \( S \).
\end{thm}

\begin{cor}
Corollary 2.3.1.
The convex hull of a finite subset \( \{b_0, \ldots, b_m\} \) of \( \mathbb{R}^n \) consists of all the vectors of the form \( \lambda_0 b_0 + \cdots + \lambda_m b_m \), with \( \lambda_0 \geq 0, \ldots, \lambda_m \geq 0, \lambda_0 + \cdots + \lambda_m = 1 \).
\end{cor}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Proof.
Every convex combination of elements selected from \( \{b_0, \ldots, b_m\} \) can be expressed as a convex combination of \( b_0, \ldots, b_m \) by including the unneeded vectors \( b_i \) with zero coefficients.
\end{proof}

content:
\begin{thm}
THEOREM 2.4. The dimension of a convex set \( C \) is the maximum of the dimensions of the various simplices included in \( C \).
\end{thm}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\textbf{PROOF.} The convex hull of any subset of \( C \) is included in \( C \). The maximum dimension of the various simplices included in \( C \) is thus the largest \( m \) such that \( C \) contains an affinely independent set of \( m + 1 \) elements. Let \(\{b_0, b_1, \ldots, b_m\}\) be such a set with \( m \) maximal, and let \( M \) be its affine hull. Then \( \dim M = m \) and \( M \subset \text{aff } C \). Furthermore \( C \subset M \), for if \( C \setminus M \) contained an element \( b \), the set of \( m + 2 \) elements \( b_0, \ldots, b_m \), \( b \) in \( C \) would be affinely independent, contrary to the maximality of \( m \). (Namely, \( \text{aff } \{b_0, \ldots, b_m, b\} \) would include \( M \) properly and hence would be more than \( m \)-dimensional.) Since \( \text{aff } C \) is the smallest affine set which includes \( C \), it follows that \( \text{aff } C = M \) and hence that \( \dim C = m \).
\end{proof}

