Proof. By Theorem 2.6, \( K \) is closed under addition and positive scalar multiplication. To be a subspace, a set must further contain 0 and be closed under multiplication by \(-1\). Clearly \( K - K \) is the smallest such set containing \( K \), and \( (-K) \cap K \) is the largest such set contained within \( K \). The former must coincide with aff \( K \), since the affine hull of a set containing 0 is a subspace by Theorem 1.1.
