\begin{thm}
Theorem 3.6. Let \( C_1 \) and \( C_2 \) be convex sets in \( R^{m+p} \), and let \( C \) be the set of vectors \( x = (y, z) \) (where \( y \in R^m \) and \( z \in R^p \)) such that there exist vectors \( z_1 \) and \( z_2 \) with \( (y, z_1) \in C_1 \), \( (y, z_2) \in C_2 \) and \( z_1 + z_2 = z \). Then \( C \) is a convex set in \( R^{m+p} \).
\end{thm}

\begin{thm}
Theorem 3.7. If \( C_1 \) and \( C_2 \) are convex sets in \( \mathbb{R}^n \), then so is their inverse sum \( C_1 \# C_2 \).
\end{thm}

proof:
