Proof. By the preceding remarks. \(\|\)
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Proof. By Theorem 3.3, \( \text{conv}(K_1 \cup K_2) \) is the union over \( \lambda \in [0, 1] \) of \( (1 - \lambda)K_1 + \lambda K_2 \). The latter set is \( K_1 + K_2 \) when \( 0 < \lambda < 1 \), \( K_1 \) when \( \lambda = 0 \), and \( K_2 \) when \( \lambda = 1 \). Since \( 0 \in K_1 \) and \( 0 \in K_2 \), \( K_1 + K_2 \) includes both \( K_1 \) and \( K_2 \). Thus \( \text{conv}(K_1 \cup K_2) \) coincides with \( K_1 + K_2 \). Similarly, \( K_1 \# K_2 \) is the union over \( \lambda \in [0, 1] \) of \( (\lambda K_1) \cap (1 - \lambda)K_2 \). The latter set is \( K_1 \cap K_2 \) when \( 0 < \lambda < 1 \), and it is \( \{0\} \subset K_1 \cap K_2 \) when \( \lambda = 0 \) or \( \lambda = 1 \). Thus \( K_1 \# K_2 = K_1 \cap K_2 \).
\end{proof}

