\begin{thm}
Theorem 4.4. Let \( f \) be a twice continuously differentiable real-valued function on an open interval \(( \alpha, \beta )\). Then \( f \) is convex if and only if its second derivative \( f'' \) is non-negative throughout \(( \alpha, \beta )\).
\end{thm}

\begin{thm}
Theorem 4.5. Let \(f\) be a twice continuously differentiable real-valued function on an open convex set \(C\) in \(\mathbb{R}^n\). Then \(f\) is convex on \(C\) if and only if its Hessian matrix

\[ Q_x = (q_{ij}(x)), \quad q_{ij}(x) = \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial \xi_i \partial \xi_j} (\xi_1, \ldots, \xi_n), \]

is positive semi-definite for every \(x \in C\).
\end{thm}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Proof. The convexity of \(f\) on \(C\) is equivalent to the convexity of the restriction of \(f\) to each line segment in \(C\). This is the same as the convexity of the function \(g(\lambda) = f(y + \lambda z)\) on the open real interval \(\{\lambda | y + \lambda z \in C\}\) for each \(y \in C\) and \(z \in \mathbb{R}^n\). A straightforward calculation shows that

\[ g''(\lambda) = \langle z, Q_x z \rangle, \quad x = y + \lambda z. \]

Thus, by Theorem 4.4, \(g\) is convex for each \(y \in C\) and \(z \in \mathbb{R}^n\) if and only if \(\langle z, Q_x z \rangle \geq 0\) for every \(z \in \mathbb{R}^n\) and \(x \in C\). \(\|\)
\end{proof}

